"**Final Verdict:** The solution is **invalid**. It contains a **Critical Error** by misinterpreting the problem (restricting to the single case \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\) instead of determining all admissible \\(k\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\)), and it also has several **Justification Gaps** in the case\u2011by\u2011case analysis of the \\(n=3\\) sub\u2011problem.\n\n---\n\n### List of Findings\n* **Location:** \u201cLet \\(n=3\\) \u2026 we are asked whether there exist three distinct lines \u2026 exactly two of the lines are sunny.\u201d\n  * **Issue:** **Critical Error** \u2013 The original problem asks for *all* non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) that work for *any* given \\(n\\ge3\\). The solution arbitrarily fixes \\(n=3\\) and \\(k=2\\), thereby solving a different (and far more limited) problem.\n\n* **Location:** \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=y=1\\), then the three points \\((1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\) remain uncovered. Any line joining two of these three points has slope \u2026 all of which are forbidden.\u201d\n  * **Issue:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 The claim that *every* line through any two of the three uncovered points has a forbidden slope is asserted without a systematic check (the slopes are not actually computed for each pair, nor is it shown that no other line through a single uncovered point could be sunny).\n\n* **Location:** \u201cAll pairs among these points have slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently the two sunny lines cannot both be sunny while covering them.\u201d\n  * **Issue:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 The argument that the two remaining lines cannot be sunny is not rigorously justified; it assumes without proof that any line covering any two of the uncovered points must have a forbidden slope.\n\n* **Location:** Similar statements in the vertical\u2011line and diagonal\u2011line cases (e.g., \u201cAll pairs among these four points have slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) \u2026\u201d).\n  * **Issue:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 The same lack of explicit verification of slopes appears repeatedly.\n\n* **Location:** The entire solution ends with the statement \u201cFor \\(n=3\\) the integer \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\u201d\n  * **Issue:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 The solution never addresses the other possible values of \\(k\\) for \\(n=3\\) (e.g. \\(k=0,1,3\\)) nor does it treat any \\(n>3\\). Hence it fails to determine *all* admissible \\(k\\) as required.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\nBelow each quoted fragment of the solution is a step\u2011by\u2011step assessment.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Definition of the point set \\(\\mathcal P\\)\n\n> \u201cLet  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n> \\]\n> be the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the lines.\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** Correct. This is precisely the set described in the problem statement.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Specialisation to \\(n=3\\)\n\n> \u201cFor \\(n=3\\) we have \\(n+1=4\\) and therefore  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal P=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}. \\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** The enumeration of \\(\\mathcal P\\) for \\(n=3\\) is accurate. However, the solution **does not justify** why it is sufficient to consider only the case \\(n=3\\). The original problem asks for *all* admissible \\(k\\) for *any* integer \\(n\\ge3\\). No argument is provided that the behaviour for \\(n=3\\) determines the behaviour for larger \\(n\\). This omission is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Definition of \u201csunny\u201d\n\n> \u201cA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** Correct definition, matching the problem\u2019s description.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Misinterpretation of the problem\n\n> \u201cWe are asked whether there exist three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) such that  \n> * every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and  \n> * exactly two of the lines are sunny.\u201d\n\n**Issue:** The original problem does **not** fix the number of lines to three nor the number of sunny lines to two. It asks, for a *given* \\(n\\ge3\\), which integers \\(k\\) can occur among the \\(n\\) lines. By restricting to the specific case \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\) the author solves a *different* problem. This is a **Critical Error** because the entire argument is built on an incorrect premise, rendering the solution irrelevant to the posed question.\n\n*Consequences:* All subsequent reasoning, even if correct for the sub\u2011problem, does not address the actual question. Therefore the solution cannot be considered valid.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Classification of non\u2011sunny line types\n\n> \u201cA non\u2011sunny line is of one of the following three types:  \n> * **Type H** (horizontal): \\(y=c\\);  \n> * **Type V** (vertical): \\(x=c\\);  \n> * **Type D** (diagonal of slope \\(-1\\)): \\(x+y=c\\).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** Correct classification of lines that are parallel to the forbidden directions.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Case 1 \u2013 Horizontal non\u2011sunny line\n\n> \u201cThe only horizontal lines that contain points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are  \n> \\[\n> y=1\\;( \\text{contains }(1,1),(2,1),(3,1)),\\qquad\n> y=2\\;( \\text{contains }(1,2),(2,2)),\\qquad\n> y=3\\;( \\text{contains }(1,3)).\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** Accurate identification of horizontal lines intersecting \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n#### 6.1 Sub\u2011case \\(L_{1}=y=1\\)\n\n> \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=y=1\\), then the three points \\((1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\) remain uncovered.  \n> Any line joining two of these three points has slope  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{2-2}{1-2}=0,\\qquad\n> \\frac{2-3}{1-1}=\\infty,\\qquad\n> \\frac{2-3}{2-1}=-1,\n> \\]  \n> all of which are forbidden. Hence the two remaining lines cannot both be sunny.\u201d\n\n**Issue:** The author lists three slopes, each corresponding to a *specific* pair of points:\n- \\((1,2)-(2,2)\\) gives slope \\(0\\);\n- \\((1,2)-(1,3)\\) gives slope \\(\\infty\\);\n- \\((2,2)-(1,3)\\) gives slope \\(-1\\).\n\nThese are indeed the only three unordered pairs among the three uncovered points, so the claim that *any* line through any two uncovered points has a forbidden slope is correct. However, the solution does **not** explicitly state that these are the *only* possible pairs; it merely says \u201cany line joining two of these three points has slope \u2026\u201d. While the reader can infer the enumeration, a rigorous proof would list all three pairs. The omission is a **Justification Gap**, albeit a minor one.\n\n#### 6.2 Sub\u2011case \\(L_{1}=y=2\\)\n\n> \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=y=2\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,1),(3,1),(1,3),(2,1)\\).  \n> Again every pair among these points has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently no sunny line can contain two of them, and we have only two sunny lines for four points \u2013 impossible.\u201d\n\n**Issue:** The author asserts that *every* pair among the four points has a forbidden slope, but does not provide the explicit slope calculations. Let us verify:\n\nUncovered points: A=(1,1), B=(3,1), C=(1,3), D=(2,1).\n\nPairs and slopes:\n- A\u2013B: slope \\((1-1)/(3-1)=0\\) (forbidden).\n- A\u2013C: slope \\((3-1)/(1-1)=\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A\u2013D: slope \\((1-1)/(2-1)=0\\) (forbidden).\n- B\u2013C: slope \\((3-1)/(1-3)=2/(-2)=-1\\) (forbidden).\n- B\u2013D: slope \\((1-1)/(2-3)=0/(-1)=0\\) (forbidden).\n- C\u2013D: slope \\((1-3)/(2-1)=-2\\) (this is **not** forbidden! The slope \\(-2\\) is allowed (it is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\)). Thus the claim that *every* pair has a forbidden slope is false.\n\nHence the statement \u201cevery pair \u2026 has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)\u201d is **incorrect**. This is a **Critical Error** in the sub\u2011case analysis, because the existence of a non\u2011forbidden slope \\(-2\\) means that a sunny line could pass through points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\). The subsequent conclusion that \u201cno sunny line can contain two of them\u201d is therefore invalid.\n\nBecause this error occurs in a sub\u2011case that the author uses to rule out the horizontal non\u2011sunny line \\(y=2\\), the argument that \u201ca horizontal non\u2011sunny line cannot lead to a valid configuration\u201d is not fully justified. However, the overall conclusion (that no configuration with a horizontal non\u2011sunny line works) may still hold after a correct analysis, but the presented reasoning is flawed. This is a **Critical Error** in the proof.\n\n#### 6.3 Sub\u2011case \\(L_{1}=y=3\\)\n\n> \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=y=3\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n> All pairs among these five points have forbidden slopes (the same calculation as above), so the two sunny lines cannot cover them all.\u201d\n\n**Issue:** The author again claims that *all* pairs have forbidden slopes without verification. Let us check:\n\nUncovered points: A=(1,1), B=(2,1), C=(3,1), D=(1,2), E=(2,2).\n\nCompute slopes:\n- A\u2013B: slope 0 (forbidden).\n- A\u2013C: slope 0 (forbidden).\n- A\u2013D: slope \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A\u2013E: slope 1 (allowed!).\n- B\u2013C: slope 0 (forbidden).\n- B\u2013D: slope \\((2-1)/(1-2)=1/(-1)=-1\\) (forbidden).\n- B\u2013E: slope \\((2-1)/(2-2)=1/0=\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- C\u2013D: slope \\((2-1)/(1-3)=1/(-2)=-0.5\\) (allowed!).\n- C\u2013E: slope \\((2-1)/(2-3)=1/(-1)=-1\\) (forbidden).\n- D\u2013E: slope 0 (forbidden).\n\nThus there are at least two pairs with allowed slopes: (1,1)-(2,2) (slope 1) and (3,1)-(1,2) (slope \\(-1/2\\)). Therefore the claim that \u201call pairs \u2026 have forbidden slopes\u201d is false. This is another **Critical Error**.\n\nBecause the author\u2019s conclusion that a horizontal non\u2011sunny line cannot work relies on this false claim, the argument is invalid for the sub\u2011case \\(y=3\\). The correct analysis would need to consider the possibility of using a sunny line of slope 1 or \\(-1/2\\) to cover two of the uncovered points, which the author completely overlooks.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Case 2 \u2013 Vertical non\u2011sunny line\n\n> \u201cThe vertical lines that meet \\(\\mathcal P\\) are \u2026\u201d\n\nThe enumeration of vertical lines is correct.\n\n#### 7.1 Sub\u2011case \\(L_{1}=x=1\\)\n\n> \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=x=1\\), the uncovered points are \\((2,1),(3,1),(2,2),(1,2)\\).  \n> Every pair among these four points has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore the two sunny lines cannot cover them.\u201d\n\n**Issue:** Again the author asserts that *every* pair has a forbidden slope without verification. Let us check:\n\nUncovered points: A=(2,1), B=(3,1), C=(2,2), D=(1,2).\n\nPairs:\n- A\u2013B: slope 0 (forbidden).\n- A\u2013C: slope \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A\u2013D: slope \\((2-1)/(1-2)=1/(-1)=-1\\) (forbidden).\n- B\u2013C: slope \\((2-1)/(2-3)=1/(-1)=-1\\) (forbidden).\n- B\u2013D: slope \\((2-1)/(1-3)=1/(-2)=-0.5\\) (allowed!).\n- C\u2013D: slope 0 (forbidden).\n\nThus the pair (3,1)-(1,2) has slope \\(-1/2\\), which is allowed. Hence the claim is false. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n#### 7.2 Sub\u2011case \\(L_{1}=x=2\\)\n\n> \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=x=2\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n> Again each pair has a forbidden slope, so two sunny lines are insufficient.\u201d\n\n**Issue:** Verify:\n\nUncovered points: A=(1,1), B=(1,2), C=(1,3), D=(3,1).\n\nPairs:\n- A\u2013B: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A\u2013C: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A\u2013D: slope \\((1-1)/(3-1)=0\\) (forbidden).\n- B\u2013C: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- B\u2013D: slope \\((1-2)/(3-1)=-1/2\\) (allowed!).\n- C\u2013D: slope \\((1-3)/(3-1)=-2/2=-1\\) (forbidden).\n\nThus the pair (1,2)-(3,1) has slope \\(-1/2\\), a sunny slope. The author\u2019s claim is false \u2192 **Critical Error**.\n\n#### 7.3 Sub\u2011case \\(L_{1}=x=3\\)\n\n> \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=x=3\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2)\\).  \n> The same slope calculation shows that no sunny line can contain two of these points; consequently the two sunny lines cannot cover all five points.\u201d\n\n**Issue:** Check the uncovered set: A=(1,1), B=(1,2), C=(2,1), D=(1,3), E=(2,2).\n\nPairs:\n- A\u2013B: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A\u2013C: slope 0 (forbidden).\n- A\u2013D: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A\u2013E: slope 1 (allowed!).\n- B\u2013C: slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden).\n- B\u2013D: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- B\u2013E: slope 0 (forbidden).\n- C\u2013D: slope \\(-2\\) (allowed!).\n- C\u2013E: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- D\u2013E: slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden).\n\nThus there are allowed slopes (1 and \\(-2\\)). The claim that \u201cno sunny line can contain two of these points\u201d is false \u2192 **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Case 3 \u2013 Diagonal non\u2011sunny line\n\n> \u201cFor the points in \\(\\mathcal P\\) the sums \\(a+b\\) are \u2026 Thus the possible diagonal non\u2011sunny lines are \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\).\u201d\n\nCorrect identification of the three possible diagonal lines.\n\n#### 8.1 Sub\u2011case \\(L_{1}:x+y=4\\)\n\n> \u201cIt contains the three points \\((1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\).  \n> The remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n> All three pairwise slopes among these are \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently the two sunny lines cannot both be sunny while covering them.\u201d\n\n**Issue:** Verify the slopes among the remaining points:\n\nRemaining points: A=(1,1), B=(1,2), C=(2,1).\n\nPairs:\n- A\u2013B: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A\u2013C: slope 0 (forbidden).\n- B\u2013C: slope \\((1-2)/(2-1)=-1\\) (forbidden).\n\nThus indeed all three slopes are forbidden. This sub\u2011case is correctly argued. No gap here.\n\n#### 8.2 Sub\u2011case \\(L_{1}:x+y=3\\)\n\n> \u201cIt contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n> The uncovered set is \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \n> Among these four points the only sunny pair is \\((1,1)\\) with \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n> Thus one sunny line can be \\(y=x\\) through those two points.  \n> The two points left, \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), have slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden; therefore the second sunny line would be non\u2011sunny, contradicting the requirement of exactly two sunny lines.\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** The analysis is correct:\n\n- Uncovered points: A=(1,1), B=(1,3), C=(3,1), D=(2,2).\n- Pairs:\n  - A\u2013D: slope 1 (sunny).\n  - A\u2013B: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n  - A\u2013C: 0 (forbidden).\n  - B\u2013C: \\(-1\\) (forbidden).\n  - B\u2013D: \\(-1\\) (forbidden).\n  - C\u2013D: \\(-1\\) (forbidden).\n\nThus the only possible sunny line covering two uncovered points is \\(y=x\\) (A\u2013D). After using that line, the remaining points B and C have slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden, so they cannot be covered by a sunny line. Hence the configuration fails. This sub\u2011case is correctly handled.\n\n#### 8.3 Sub\u2011case \\(L_{1}:x+y=2\\)\n\n> \u201cIt contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n> The five remaining points must be covered by two sunny lines, so one of those lines would have to contain at least three points.  \n> A direct check of all triples among the five points shows that no three are collinear with a sunny slope (every triple contains a pair of slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \n> Hence two sunny lines cannot cover the five points.\u201d\n\n**Issue:** The author claims \u201ca direct check of all triples \u2026 shows that no three are collinear with a sunny slope\u201d. However, the check is not shown. Let us verify quickly:\n\nRemaining points: (1,2), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1), (2,2). We need to see if any three of them lie on a line with slope not in \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\nList all triples (there are \\(\\binom{5}{3}=10\\) triples). We can check each:\n\n1. (1,2), (2,1), (1,3): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(2,1): -1 (forbidden)\n   - (1,2)-(1,3): \u221e (forbidden)\n   - (2,1)-(1,3): -2 (allowed) but the three points are not collinear because slopes differ. So not collinear.\n\n2. (1,2), (2,1), (3,1): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(2,1): -1 (forbidden)\n   - (1,2)-(3,1): (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 (allowed)\n   - (2,1)-(3,1): 0 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n3. (1,2), (2,1), (2,2): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(2,1): -1 (forbidden)\n   - (1,2)-(2,2): 0 (forbidden)\n   - (2,1)-(2,2): \u221e (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n4. (1,2), (1,3), (3,1): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(1,3): \u221e (forbidden)\n   - (1,2)-(3,1): -1/2 (allowed)\n   - (1,3)-(3,1): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n5. (1,2), (1,3), (2,2): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(1,3): \u221e (forbidden)\n   - (1,2)-(2,2): 0 (forbidden)\n   - (1,3)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n6. (1,2), (3,1), (2,2): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(3,1): -1/2 (allowed)\n   - (1,2)-(2,2): 0 (forbidden)\n   - (3,1)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n7. (2,1), (1,3), (3,1): slopes:\n   - (2,1)-(1,3): -2 (allowed)\n   - (2,1)-(3,1): 0 (forbidden)\n   - (1,3)-(3,1): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n8. (2,1), (1,3), (2,2): slopes:\n   - (2,1)-(1,3): -2 (allowed)\n   - (2,1)-(2,2): \u221e (forbidden)\n   - (1,3)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n9. (2,1), (3,1), (2,2): slopes:\n   - (2,1)-(3,1): 0 (forbidden)\n   - (2,1)-(2,2): \u221e (forbidden)\n   - (3,1)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n10. (1,3), (3,1), (2,2): slopes:\n    - (1,3)-(3,1): -1 (forbidden)\n    - (1,3)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n    - (3,1)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n    Not collinear.\n\nThus indeed no three points are collinear on a sunny line. So the claim is correct, but the solution does not provide this verification; it merely states \u201ca direct check \u2026 shows \u2026\u201d. While the statement is true, the lack of explicit verification is a **Justification Gap** (the step is asserted without proof). However, the gap does not affect correctness if the claim is true; we note it as a gap.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Conclusion of the sub\u2011problem\n\n> \u201cSince every possible choice for the unique non\u2011sunny line results in an impossibility, there is **no** collection of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) for \\(n=3\\).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** The conclusion is based on the case analysis. However, as shown in Sections 6.2, 6.3, 7.1\u20137.3, several sub\u2011cases contain **Critical Errors** (incorrect statements about slopes). Therefore the conclusion that \u201cevery possible choice \u2026 results in an impossibility\u201d is not rigorously established. The errors could potentially affect the final conclusion. Even if the final statement happens to be true, the proof is invalid because of the critical errors in the intermediate steps. Hence this part of the argument is **invalid**.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Overall answer to the original problem\n\n> \u201c\\[\n> \\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, the integer }k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Issue:** The original problem required **all** admissible values of \\(k\\) for **any** \\(n\\ge3\\). The solution provides only a single negative result for a single pair \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\). This does not answer the problem. Hence this is a **Justification Gap** (incomplete answer).\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Issues\n\n| # | Location (quote) | Issue Type | Explanation |\n|---|------------------|------------|-------------|\n| 1 | \u201cWe are asked whether there exist three distinct lines \u2026 exactly two of the lines are sunny.\u201d | **Critical Error** | Misinterprets the problem; the original asks for all admissible \\(k\\) for any given \\(n\\), not just the case \\(n=3, k=2\\). |\n| 2 | \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=y=2\\)\u2026 every pair among these points has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\u201d | **Critical Error** | The pair \\((1,3)-(2,1)\\) has slope \\(-2\\), which is sunny; the claim is false. |\n| 3 | \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=y=3\\)\u2026 All pairs among these five points have forbidden slopes.\u201d | **Critical Error** | Counterexample: (1,1)-(2,2) has slope 1 (sunny); (3,1)-(1,2) has slope \\(-1/2\\) (sunny). |\n| 4 | \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=x=1\\)\u2026 Every pair among these four points has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\u201d | **Critical Error** | Pair (3,1)-(1,2) has slope \\(-1/2\\) (sunny). |\n| 5 | \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=x=2\\)\u2026 again each pair has a forbidden slope.\u201d | **Critical Error** | Pair (1,2)-(3,1) has slope \\(-1/2\\) (sunny). |\n| 6 | \u201cIf \\(L_{1}=x=3\\)\u2026 no sunny line can contain two of these points.\u201d | **Critical Error** | Pairs (1,1)-(2,2) (slope 1) and (2,1)-(1,3) (slope \\(-2\\)) are sunny. |\n| 7 | \u201cAll three pairwise slopes among these are \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently the two sunny lines cannot both be sunny while covering them.\u201d (in diagonal case \\(x+y=4\\)) | **Correct** | The claim is true; no gap. |\n| 8 | \u201cAmong these four points the only sunny pair is \\((1,1)\\) with \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).\u201d (diagonal case \\(x+y=3\\)) | **Correct** | Accurate. |\n| 9 | \u201cA direct check of all triples \u2026 shows that no three are collinear with a sunny slope.\u201d (diagonal case \\(x+y=2\\)) | **Justification Gap** | The claim is true but not demonstrated; the step is asserted without proof. |\n|10 | \u201cSince every possible choice \u2026 there is **no** collection \u2026 for \\(n=3\\).\u201d | **Critical Error** | Relies on the flawed sub\u2011cases (2\u20136); the conclusion is not rigorously established. |\n|11 | Final answer only addresses \\(n=3, k=2\\). | **Justification Gap** | Does not determine all admissible \\(k\\) for any \\(n\\ge3\\); incomplete solution. |\n\n---\n\n### Overall Conclusion\n\nThe solution fails to address the problem as stated (Critical Error) and contains multiple incorrect statements about slopes (Critical Errors) that invalidate the case analysis. Additionally, several steps are asserted without proof (Justification Gaps). Consequently, the solution is **invalid**."

