t one of the three lines, and  \n> * exactly two of the lines are sunny.”\n\n**Issue:** The original problem does **not** fix the number of lines to three nor the number of sunny lines to two. It asks, for a *given* \\(n\\ge3\\), which integers \\(k\\) can occur among the \\(n\\) lines. By restricting to the specific case \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\) the author solves a *different* problem. This is a **Critical Error** because the entire argument is built on an incorrect premise, rendering the solution irrelevant to the posed question.\n\n*Consequences:* All subsequent reasoning, even if correct for the sub‑problem, does not address the actual question. Therefore the solution cannot be considered valid.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Classification of non‑sunny line types\n\n> “A non‑sunny line is of one of the following three types:  \n> * **Type H** (horizontal): \\(y=c\\);  \n> * **Type V** (vertical): \\(x=c\\);  \n> * **Type D** (diagonal of slope \\(-1\\)): \\(x+y=c\\).”\n\n**Assessment:** Correct classification of lines that are parallel to the forbidden directions.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Case 1 – Horizontal non‑sunny line\n\n> “The only horizontal lines that contain points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) are  \n> \\[\n> y=1\\;( \\text{contains }(1,1),(2,1),(3,1)),\\qquad\n> y=2\\;( \\text{contains }(1,2),(2,2)),\\qquad\n> y=3\\;( \\text{contains }(1,3)).\n> \\]”\n\n**Assessment:** Accurate identification of horizontal lines intersecting \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n#### 6.1 Sub‑case \\(L_{1}=y=1\\)\n\n> “If \\(L_{1}=y=1\\), then the three points \\((1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\) remain uncovered.  \n> Any line joining two of these three points has slope  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{2-2}{1-2}=0,\\qquad\n> \\frac{2-3}{1-1}=\\infty,\\qquad\n> \\frac{2-3}{2-1}=-1,\n> \\]  \n> all of which are forbidden. Hence the two remaining lines cannot both be sunny.”\n\n**Issue:** The author lists three slopes, each corresponding to a *specific* pair of points:\n- \\((1,2)-(2,2)\\) gives slope \\(0\\);\n- \\((1,2)-(1,3)\\) gives slope \\(\\infty\\);\n- \\((2,2)-(1,3)\\) gives slope \\(-1\\).\n\nThese are indeed the only three unordered pairs among the three uncovered points, so the claim that *any* line through any two uncovered points has a forbidden slope is correct. However, the solution does **not** explicitly state that these are the *only* possible pairs; it merely says “any line joining two of these three points has slope …”. While the reader can infer the enumeration, a rigorous proof would list all three pairs. The omission is a **Justification Gap**, albeit a minor one.\n\n#### 6.2 Sub‑case \\(L_{1}=y=2\\)\n\n> “If \\(L_{1}=y=2\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,1),(3,1),(1,3),(2,1)\\).  \n> Again every pair among these points has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently no sunny line can contain two of them, and we have only two sunny lines for four points – impossible.”\n\n**Issue:** The author asserts that *every* pair among the four points has a forbidden slope, but does not provide the explicit slope calculations. Let us verify:\n\nUncovered points: A=(1,1), B=(3,1), C=(1,3), D=(2,1).\n\nPairs and slopes:\n- A–B: slope \\((1-1)/(3-1)=0\\) (forbidden).\n- A–C: slope \\((3-1)/(1-1)=\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A–D: slope \\((1-1)/(2-1)=0\\) (forbidden).\n- B–C: slope \\((3-1)/(1-3)=2/(-2)=-1\\) (forbidden).\n- B–D: slope \\((1-1)/(2-3)=0/(-1)=0\\) (forbidden).\n- C–D: slope \\((1-3)/(2-1)=-2\\) (this is **not** forbidden! The slope \\(-2\\) is allowed (it is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\)). Thus the claim that *every* pair has a forbidden slope is false.\n\nHence the statement “every pair … has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)” is **incorrect**. This is a **Critical Error** in the sub‑case analysis, because the existence of a non‑forbidden slope \\(-2\\) means that a sunny line could pass through points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\). The subsequent conclusion that “no sunny line can contain two of them” is therefore invalid.\n\nBecause this error occurs in a sub‑case that