## 7.1 Sub‑case \\(L_{1}=x=1\\)\n\n> “If \\(L_{1}=x=1\\), the uncovered points are \\((2,1),(3,1),(2,2),(1,2)\\).  \n> Every pair among these four points has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore the two sunny lines cannot cover them.”\n\n**Issue:** Again the author asserts that *every* pair has a forbidden slope without verification. Let us check:\n\nUncovered points: A=(2,1), B=(3,1), C=(2,2), D=(1,2).\n\nPairs:\n- A–B: slope 0 (forbidden).\n- A–C: slope \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A–D: slope \\((2-1)/(1-2)=1/(-1)=-1\\) (forbidden).\n- B–C: slope \\((2-1)/(2-3)=1/(-1)=-1\\) (forbidden).\n- B–D: slope \\((2-1)/(1-3)=1/(-2)=-0.5\\) (allowed!).\n- C–D: slope 0 (forbidden).\n\nThus the pair (3,1)-(1,2) has slope \\(-1/2\\), which is allowed. Hence the claim is false. This is a **Critical Error**.\n\n#### 7.2 Sub‑case \\(L_{1}=x=2\\)\n\n> “If \\(L_{1}=x=2\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n> Again each pair has a forbidden slope, so two sunny lines are insufficient.”\n\n**Issue:** Verify:\n\nUncovered points: A=(1,1), B=(1,2), C=(1,3), D=(3,1).\n\nPairs:\n- A–B: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A–C: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A–D: slope \\((1-1)/(3-1)=0\\) (forbidden).\n- B–C: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- B–D: slope \\((1-2)/(3-1)=-1/2\\) (allowed!).\n- C–D: slope \\((1-3)/(3-1)=-2/2=-1\\) (forbidden).\n\nThus the pair (1,2)-(3,1) has slope \\(-1/2\\), a sunny slope. The author’s claim is false → **Critical Error**.\n\n#### 7.3 Sub‑case \\(L_{1}=x=3\\)\n\n> “If \\(L_{1}=x=3\\), the uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2)\\).  \n> The same slope calculation shows that no sunny line can contain two of these points; consequently the two sunny lines cannot cover all five points.”\n\n**Issue:** Check the uncovered set: A=(1,1), B=(1,2), C=(2,1), D=(1,3), E=(2,2).\n\nPairs:\n- A–B: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A–C: slope 0 (forbidden).\n- A–D: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A–E: slope 1 (allowed!).\n- B–C: slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden).\n- B–D: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- B–E: slope 0 (forbidden).\n- C–D: slope \\(-2\\) (allowed!).\n- C–E: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- D–E: slope \\(-1\\) (forbidden).\n\nThus there are allowed slopes (1 and \\(-2\\)). The claim that “no sunny line can contain two of these points” is false → **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Case 3 – Diagonal non‑sunny line\n\n> “For the points in \\(\\mathcal P\\) the sums \\(a+b\\) are … Thus the possible diagonal non‑sunny lines are \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\).”\n\nCorrect identification of the three possible diagonal lines.\n\n#### 8.1 Sub‑case \\(L_{1}:x+y=4\\)\n\n> “It contains the three points \\((1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\).  \n> The remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n> All three pairwise slopes among these are \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently the two sunny lines cannot both be sunny while covering them.”\n\n**Issue:** Verify the slopes among the remaining points:\n\nRemaining points: A=(1,1), B=(1,2), C=(2,1).\n\nPairs:\n- A–B: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n- A–C: slope 0 (forbidden).\n- B–C: slope \\((1-2)/(2-1)=-1\\) (forbidden).\n\nThus indeed all three slopes are forbidden. This sub‑case is correctly argued. No gap here.\n\n#### 8.2 Sub‑case \\(L_{1}:x+y=3\\)\n\n> “It contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n> The uncovered set is \\(\\{(1,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \n> Among these four points the only sunny pair is \\((1,1)\\) with \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n> Thus one sunny line can be \\(y=x\\) through those two points.  \n> The two points left, \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), have slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden; therefore the second sunny line would be non‑sunny, contradicting the requirement of exactly two sunny lines.”\n\n**Assessment:** The analysis is correct:\n\n- Uncovered points: A=(1,1), B=(1,3), C=(3,1), D=(2,2).\n- Pairs:\n  - A–D: slope 1 (sunny).\n  - A–B: \\(\\infty\\) (forbidden).\n  - A–C: 0 (forbidden).\n  - B–C: \\(-1\\) (forbidden).\n  - B–D: \\(-1\\) (forbidden).\n  - C–D: \\(-1\\) (forbidden).\n\nThus the only possible sunny line covering two uncovered points is \\(y=x\\) (A–D). After using that line, the remaining points B and C have slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden, so they cannot be covered by a sunny line. Hence the configuration fails. This sub‑case is correctly handled.\n\n#### 8.3 Sub‑case \\(L_{1}:x+y=2\\)\n\n> “It contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n> The five remaining points must be covered by two sunny lines, so one of those lines would have to contain at least three points.  \n> A direct check of all triples among the five points shows that no three are collinear with a sunny slope (every triple contains a pair of slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \n> Hence two sunny lines cannot cover the five points.”\n\n**Issue:** The author claims “a direct check of all triples … shows that no three are collinear with a sunny slope”. However, the check is not shown. Let us verify quickly:\n\nRemaining points: (1,2), (2,1), (1,3), (3,1), (2,2). We need to see if any three of them lie on a line with slope not in \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\nList all triples (there are \\(\\binom{5}{3}=10\\) triples). We can check each:\n\n1. (1,2), (2,1), (1,3): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(2,1): -1 (forbidden)\n   - (1,2)-(1,3): ∞ (forbidden)\n   - (2,1)-(1,3): -2 (allowed) but the three points are not collinear because slopes differ. So not collinear.\n\n2. (1,2), (2,1), (3,1): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(2,1): -1 (forbidden)\n   - (1,2)-(3,1): (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 (allowed)\n   - (2,1)-(3,1): 0 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n3. (1,2), (2,1), (2,2): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(2,1): -1 (forbidden)\n   - (1,2)-(2,2): 0 (forbidden)\n   - (2,1)-(2,2): ∞ (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n4. (1,2), (1,3), (3,1): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(1,3): ∞ (forbidden)\n   - (1,2)-(3,1): -1/2 (allowed)\n   - (1,3)-(3,1): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n5. (1,2), (1,3), (2,2): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(1,3): ∞ (forbidden)\n   - (1,2)-(2,2): 0 (forbidden)\n   - (1,3)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n6. (1,2), (3,1), (2,2): slopes:\n   - (1,2)-(3,1): -1/2 (allowed)\n   - (1,2)-(2,2): 0 (forbidden)\n   - (3,1)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n7. (2,1), (1,3), (3,1): slopes:\n   - (2,1)-(1,3): -2 (allowed)\n   - (2,1)-(3,1): 0 (forbidden)\n   - (1,3)-(3,1): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n8. (2,1), (1,3), (2,2): slopes:\n   - (2,1)-(1,3): -2 (allowed)\n   - (2,1)-(2,2): ∞ (forbidden)\n   - (1,3)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n9. (2,1), (3,1), (2,2): slopes:\n   - (2,1)-(3,1): 0 (forbidden)\n   - (2,1)-(2,2): ∞ (forbidden)\n   - (3,1)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n   Not collinear.\n\n10. (1,3), (3,1), (2,2): slopes:\n    - (1,3)-(3,1): -1 (forbidden)\n    - (1,3)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n    - (3,1)-(2,2): -1 (forbidden)\n    Not collinear.\n\nThus indeed no three points are collinear on a sunny line. So the claim is correct, but the solution does not provide this verification; it merely states “a direct check … shows …”. While the statement is true, the lack of explicit verification is a **Justification Gap** (the step is asserted without proof). However, the gap does not affect correctness if the claim is true; we note it as a gap.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Conclusion of the sub‑problem\n\n> “Since every possible choice for the unique non‑sunny line results in an impossibility, there is **no** collection of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) for \\(n=3\\).”\n\n**Assessment:** The conclusion is based on the case analysis. However, as shown in Sections 6.2, 6.3, 7.1–7.3, several sub‑cases contain **Critical Errors** (incorrect statements about slopes). Therefore the conclusion that “every possible choice … results in an impossibility” is not rigorously established. The errors could potentially affect the final conclusion. Even if the final statement happens to be true, the proof is invalid because of the critical errors in the intermediate steps. Hence this part of