(1,2),(1,3)\\);\n* \\(\\ell_{2}\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\);\n* \\(\\ell_{3}\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nThe union of the three sets is exactly \\(S\\).  \nThe three lines are distinct because their slopes are \\( \\infty,0,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(k=1\\) (one sunny line, two non‑sunny)\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:x=1,\\qquad \n\\ell_{2}:x+y=4,\\qquad \n\\ell_{3}:y=-2x+5 .\n\\]\n\n* \\(\\ell_{1}\\in\\mathcal V\\) and \\(\\ell_{2}\\in\\mathcal D\\) are non‑sunny;  \n  \\(\\ell_{3}\\) has slope \\(-2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence it is sunny.*\n\nCoverage:\n\n* \\(\\ell_{1}\\) supplies \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\);\n* \\(\\ell_{2}\\) supplies \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\);\n* \\(\\ell_{3}\\) passes through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) (the latter already covered).\n\nThus every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the three lines.\nThe three lines are distinct because their slopes are \\(\\infty,-1,-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(k=2\\) (two sunny lines, one non‑sunny)\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:x+y=4,\\qquad \n\\ell_{2}:y=x,\\qquad \n\\ell_{3}:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 .\n\\]\n\n* \\(\\ell_{1}\\in\\mathcal D\\) is non‑sunny;  \n  \\(\\ell_{2}\\) (slope \\(1\\)) and \\(\\ell_{3}\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) are sunny.*\n\nCoverage:\n\n* \\(\\ell_{1}\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\);\n* \\(\\ell_{2}\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,2)\\);\n* \\(\\ell_{3}\\) contains \\((1,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nAll six points of \\(S\\) appear in the union.  \nThe three slopes are \\(-1,1,-\\tfrac12\\); hence the lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  \\(k=3\\) (all three lines sunny)\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:y=x,\\qquad \n\\ell_{2}:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad \n\\ell_{3}:y=-2x+5 .\n\\]\n\n*Each line has slope \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all three are sunny.*\n\nCoverage:\n\n* \\(\\