--------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A bound for non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(b=c\\) (\\(c\\ge 1\\)).\nThe admissible points satisfy \\(c\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), whence \\(a\\le n+1-c\\).\nThus such a line contains at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nA line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(a=c\\)) is analogous.  \n\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).\nThe number of points on it is \\(c-1\\le n\\).  \n\nHence **every non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nSuppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nUsing the bounds from Sections\u202f1 and\u202f2 we obtain\n\n\\[\nkM+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;N . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert the values of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) from (1)\u2013(2).\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) is even*, then \\(M=t\\) and \\(N=t(2t+1)\\).  \nInequality (3) becomes  \n\n\\[\nkt+(2t-k)2t\\ge t(2t+1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow 4t^{2}-kt\\ge 2t^{2}+t\n\\Longleftrightarrow k\\le 2t-1=n-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*, then \\(M=t+1\\) and \\(N=(2t+1)(t+1)\\).  \nInequality (3) reads  \n\n\\[\nk(t+1)+(2t+1-k)(2t+1)\\ge (2t+1)(t+1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow (2t+1)^{2}-k\\le (2t+1)(t+1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow k\\le 2t+1=n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible family must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le \n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Constructions attaining all admissible values  \n\nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le k_{\\max}\\) (where\n\\(k_{\\max}\\) is the right\u2011hand side of (6)) can actually be realized.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=c,\\qquad c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nEach line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non\u2011sunny) and contains exactly\n\\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Their union is \\(S_n\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is possible.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.2  The maximal number of sunny lines  \n\n*Even \\(n=2t\\).*  \nConsider the \\(n-1=2t-1\\) lines of slope \\(1\\) given by  \n\n\\[\na-b=d,\\qquad d=-t+1,-t+2,\\dots ,t-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(d\\) the line contains exactly \\(t-|d|+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\);\nthe total number of points covered by the \\(2t-1\\) lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=-(t-1)}^{t-1}\\bigl(t-|d|+1\\bigr)=2t^{2}+t=N .\n\\]\n\nHence all points of \\(S_n\\) are already covered; we may add any extra line\n(e.g. \\(x=1\\)) to reach the required total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\nAll the \\(2t-1\\) lines of slope \\(1\\) are sunny, so \\(k=n-1\\) is attained.\n\n*Odd \\(n=2t+1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n=2t+1\\) lines  \n\n\\[\na-b=d,\\qquad d=-t,-t+1,\\dots ,t .\n\\]\n\nFor a given \\(d\\) the line contains \\(t+1-|d|\\) points of \\(S_n\\); summing over\nall \\(d\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=-t}^{t}\\bigl(t+1-|d|\\bigr)=(2t+1)(t+1)=N .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(n\\) lines of slope \\(1\\) already cover the whole set; they are all\nsunny, giving \\(k=n\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 4.3  Intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(1\\le k\\le k_{\\max}\\).\n\n*Even \\(n=2t\\).*  \nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct lines among the \\(2t-1\\) sunny lines used in\n\u00a74.2 (for instance the lines with \\(d=-t+1,\\dots , -t+k\\)).  \nThese \\(k\\) lines together contain at most \\(k\\,t\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\nThe remaining points, whose number is  \n\n\\[\nN-k\\,t = t(2t+1)-k\\,t = (2t-k)t + t,\n\\]\n\ncan be covered by the following \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* one vertical line \\(x=1\\) (contains \\(2t\\) points),\n* \\(n-k-1\\) lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (each of them can be chosen so as\n  to contain at most \\(t\\) of the still uncovered po