            "content": "### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have found a complete solution.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. Let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n   \\(|S_n|=N=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \n\n2. Show that any *sunny* line (i.e. a line not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\). The extremal case is a line of slope \\(1\\) (equation \\(a-b=d\\)).  \n\n3. A non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the maximal case is a vertical line \\(x=1\\) or a line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c=n+1\\)).  \n\n4. Using the bounds from (2) and (3) obtain a necessary inequality for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\):\n\n\\[\nkM+(n-k)n\\ge N .\n\\]\n\n   Solving gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le \n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\max}= \n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n5. Construct families attaining every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le k_{\\max}\\).  \n   * For \\(k=0\\) use the \\(n\\) lines \\(x+y=c\\;(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n   * For the maximal \\(k\\) use the \\(k_{\\max}\\) lines of slope \\(1\\) given by \\(a-b=d\\) with suitable integers \\(d\\); the remaining points (if any) are covered by a single non‑sunny line.  \n   * For intermediate \\(k\\) keep any \\(k\\) of those sunny lines and cover the uncovered points by a suitable collection of non‑sunny lines (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   All constructions use distinct lines, satisfy the covering condition, and contain exactly the prescribed number of sunny lines.  \n\nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is even},