tructions for\nall admissible values of \\(k\\) yields the answer stated in the verdict.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN:=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Sunny lines\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\nAll sunny lines are obtained from a line of slope \\(1\\) by an affine\ntransformation that preserves the three forbidden directions; such a\ntransformation sends the set \\(S_n\\) onto itself **only** when it is a\ntranslation parallel to the vector \\((1,1)\\).  Translation does not change\nthe number of lattice points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the line, therefore it\nsuffices to study the lines of slope \\(1\\).\n\nA line of slope \\(1\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\na-b=d\\qquad(d\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the defining inequalities of \\(S_n\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na-b=d,\\\\[2mm]\na\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\\\[2mm]\na+b\\le n+1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=b+d\\) and eliminating \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nb\\ge\\max\\{1,1-d\\},\\\\[2mm]\n2b+d\\le n+1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nHence the admissible integers \\(b\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\max\\{1,1-d\\}\\le b\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe number of solutions is therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\ell_d\\cap S_n\\bigr|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFormula (3) shows that the maximal possible cardinality of the intersection\nof a sunny line with \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(When \\(n\\) is odd the maximum is attained for \\(d=0\\); when \\(n\\) is even\nit is attained for \\(d=0\\) and also for \\(d=\\pm1\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non\u2011sunny lines\n\n*Horizontal* lines have equation \\(b=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); the\ninequalities \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a\\le n+1-c\\) show that such a line contains\nat most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Vertical* lines \\(a=c\\) are analogous.\n\nLines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) have equation \\(a+b=c\\) with\n\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); the admissible points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\), so there are exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them.\n\nConsequently every non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Using the bounds of Sections\u202f1 and\u202f2 we\nobtain\n\n\\[\nkM+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;N .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(N=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) and \\(M\\) from (4).\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) is even*, then \\(M=t\\) and (5) becomes  \n\n\\[\nkt+(2t-k)2t\\ge t(2t+1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow k\\le 2t-1=n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd*, then \\(M=t+1\\) and (5) gives  \n\n\\[\nk(t+1)+(2t+1-k)(2t+1)\\ge (2t+1)(t+1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow k\\le 2t+1=n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions\n\nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) obeying (8) can actually be\nrealised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;a+b=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non\u2011sunny) and contains\nexactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\); the union of the \\(n\\) lines is\nprecisely \\(S_n\\).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Maximal number of sunny lines\n\n*Even \\(n=2t\\).*  \nConsider the diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\;a-b=d,\\qquad d=-t+1,-t+2,\\dots ,t-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor such a \\(d\\),\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\ell_d\\cap S_n\\bigr|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2t+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =t-|d|+1 .\n\\]\n\nSumming over all \\(d\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=-(t-1)}^{t-1}(t-|d|+1)=2t^{2}+t=N .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(2t-1=n-1\\) lines already cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\nAdding any extra non\u2011sunny line (for instance the vertical line\n\\(a=1\\)) yields a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing\n\\(k=n-1\\) sunny ones.\n\n*Odd \\(n=2t+1\\).*  \nTake the diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\;a-b=d,\\qquad d=-t,-t+1,\\dots ,t .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\ell_d\\cap S_n\\bigr|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2t+2-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =t+1-|d| .\n\\]\n\nSummation yields  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=-t}^{t}(t+1-|d|)=(2t+1)(t+1)=N .\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(n\\) diagonal lines themselves form a required family with\n\\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Intermediate values \\(0<k<k_{\\max}\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (8).  \nChoose the \\(k\\) diagonal lines whose parameters \\(d\\) have the smallest\nabsolute values; explicitly\n\n\\[\nD_k:=\\Bigl\\{-\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor,\n          \\dots ,\n          \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n\\qquad |D_k|=k .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nDenote the chosen lines by \\(\\ell_d\\;(d\\in D_k)\\).  \nFrom (3) the number of points of \\(S_n\\) already covered is  \n\n\\[\nC_k:=\\sum_{d\\in D_k}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nBecause the set \\(D_k\\) consists of the \\(k\\) smallest \\(|d|\\), the\nquantity \\(C_k\\) is maximal among all possible choices of \\(k\\) diagonal\nlines; consequently inequality (5) with the maximal left\u2011hand side gives  \n\n\\[\nN-C_k\\le (n-k)n .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nTo cover the remaining \\(N-C_k\\) points we use the following\nnon\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* for each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-k\\) take the vertical line\n  \\(V_c:\\;a=c\\);\n* if after using those vertical lines some points are still uncovered,\n  they all lie on the hypotenuse \\(H:\\;a+b=n+1\\) (the line parallel to\n  \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nEach vertical line \\(V_c\\) contains at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\); the hypotenuse contains exactly \\(n\\) points.  Because of (14)\nthe total capacity of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines,\n\\((n-k)n\\), is at least the number of uncovered points, so the\ndescribed collection indeed covers all of them.  All lines are distinct:\nthe diagonal lines have slope \\(1\\), the vertical lines have infinite\nslope, and the hypotenuse has slope \\(-1\\).\n\nThus we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers\n\\(S_n\\) and contains exactly the prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (8) is also sufficient, because for every\n\\(k\\) satisfying (8) we have exhibited an explicit configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\n\\qquad \n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}\\; } .\n\\]"
