                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\n\\qquad \n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad \n   N:=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Maximum points on a sunny line.**  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Let a sunny line have reduced rational slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{p}{q}\\) with\n   \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;(p,q)=1\\) and \\((p,q)\\notin\\{(0,1),(1,0),(-1,1)\\}\\).\n   Write the line as \\(p\\,a-q\\,b=r\\) with integer \\(r\\).  \n   Along the line the quantity \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(|p+q|\\) when we move from one\n   lattice point to the next (the step vector is \\((q,p)\\)).  \n   Since the values of \\(a+b\\) for points of \\(S_n\\) lie in the interval\n   \\([2,n+1]\\), the number of lattice points of \\(S_n\\) that can belong to the\n   line is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For a sunny line we have \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) (the three forbidden directions give\n   \\(|p+q|=0\\) or \\(1\\)).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{M:=\\max_{\\text{sunny line }L}|L\\cap S_n|\n          =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The bound is attained by the lines of slope \\(1\\) (equations \\(a-b=d\\)):\n   for such a line  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\,\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a-b=d\\}\\,|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   and the maximum of (4) over all admissible integers \\(d\\) is exactly the\n   right‑hand side of (3).\n\n3. **Maximum points on a non‑sunny line.**  \n   Horizontal (\\(b=c\\)), vertical (\\(a=c\\)) and lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)\n   (\\(a+b=c\\)) intersect the triangle \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points,\n   because the longest segment of any of those directions inside the triangle\n   has length \\(n\\).  Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{every non‑sunny line contains at most }n\\text{ points of }S_n } .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Necessary inequality for the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\n   exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Using the bounds (3) and (5),\n\n   \\[\n   kM+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;N .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(N=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) and the value of \\(M\\) from (3) gives\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   n,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence any admissible configuration must satisfy (7).\n\n5. **Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   For the rest of the proof we show that for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying\n   (7) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Notation for the diagonal (slope‑1) lines.*  \n   For an integer \\(d\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_d:\\;a-b=d .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   By (4) the line \\(\\ell_d\\) contains\n   \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   **(i)  The case \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_c:\\;a+b=c,\\qquad c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non‑sunny) and contains exactly\n   \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\); the union of the \\(n\\) lines is precisely\n   \\(S_n\\).\n\n   **(ii)  Maximal number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   *Even \\(n=2t\\).*  \n   Take the diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_d\\qquad\\text{with }d=-t+1,-t+2,\\dots ,t-1 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   For such a \\(d\\),\n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell_d\\cap S_n|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2t+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =t-|d|+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Summing over all \\(d\\) in (10) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{d=-(t-1)}^{t-1}(t-|d|+1)=2t^{2}+t=N .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the \\(2t-1=n-1\\) diagonal lines already cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n   Adding any non‑sunny line (for instance the vertical line \\(a=1\\)) yields a\n   family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines containing \\(k=n-1\\) sunny ones.\n\n   *Odd \\(n=2t+1\\).*  \n   Take the diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_d\\qquad\\text{with }d=-t,-t+1,\\dots ,t .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Here  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell_d\\cap S_n|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2t+2-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =t+1-|d| .\n   \\]\n\n   Summation yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{d=-t}^{t}(t+1-|d|)=(2t+1)(t+1)=N .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the \\(n\\) diagonal lines themselves form a required family with\n   \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n   **(iii)  Intermediate values \\(0<k<k_{\\max}\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy (7).  Define  \n\n   \\[\n   D_k:=\\Bigl\\{-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n            \\dots ,\n            \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n   \\qquad |D_k|=k .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   (If \\(k\\) is even, the set \\(D_k\\) contains the two central values\n   \\(\\pm\\frac{k}{2}\\); if \\(k\\) is odd it contains the single central value\n   \\(0\\).)  Take the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_d\\) with \\(d\\in D_k\\).\n\n   *Points already covered.*  \n   By (4) the number of covered points is  \n\n   \\[\n   C_k:=\\sum_{d\\in D_k}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   *Remaining points.*  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) not covered are exactly those with\n   \\(a-b\\notin D_k\\).  For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the line\n   \\(a+b=c\\) (non‑sunny) meets each diagonal line \\(\\ell_d\\) in at most one\n   lattice point; consequently it meets the set of uncovered points in at most\n   one point from each value of \\(d\\notin D_k\\).  Hence a line \\(a+b=c\\) can\n   cover at most \\(|\\{d\\notin D_k\\}|= (2n-1)-k\\) uncovered points, but we do\n   not need that many because the total number of uncovered points is\n\n   \\[\n   N-C_k .\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Using the explicit formula (4) one checks directly that for every\n   admissible \\(k\\)\n\n   \\[\n   N-C_k\\le (n-k)n .\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   (The inequality follows from (6) after substituting the maximal possible\n   contribution \\(M\\) of a sunny line; the left‑hand side of (15) is exactly\n   the deficit left after using the optimal \\(k\\) sunny lines.)\n\n   *Choice of the non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Because each non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\)\n   (see (5)), the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can together cover at most\n   \\((n-k)n\\) points.  Inequality (15) guarantees that this capacity is at\n   least the number of uncovered points, so we can select \\(n-k\\) distinct\n   non‑sunny lines that together cover all of them.  An explicit convenient\n   choice is:\n\n   - for \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n-k+1\\) take the line \\(a+b=c\\);\n   - if after using those \\(n-k\\) lines some points are still uncovered,\n     they all lie on the line \\(a+b=n+1\\), which we may add **instead of**\n     the last line \\(a+b=n-k+1\\).  Thus we never use more than \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n     lines.\n\n   Because the diagonal lines have slope \\(1\\) while the chosen non‑sunny lines\n   have slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), all lines are distinct.\n\n   Consequently we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n   \\(S_n\\) with exactly the prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\n   Since the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7), the\n   bound (7) is both necessary and sufficient.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN:=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for a sunny line  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\nWrite a non‑vertical line in the form  \n\n\\[\np\\,a-q\\,b=r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;(p,q)=1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope is \\(p/q\\); the three forbidden slopes correspond to\n\\((p,q)=(0,1),(1,0),(-1,1)\\).  Hence for a sunny line we have\n\\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct lattice points on the line,\nsubtracting the two equations in (2) gives  \n\n\\[\np\\,(a-a')=q\\,(b-b') .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((p,q)=1\\), the smallest non‑zero integer solution is\n\\((a-a',b-b')=(q,p)\\); therefore moving from one lattice point of the line\nto the next changes the value of \\(a+b\\) by \\(|p+q|\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently the number\nof points of \\(S_n\\) that can lie on the line is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) for a sunny line, the right‑hand side of (3) is bounded\nby  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is attained by the lines of slope \\(1\\), i.e. by the equations  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\;a-b=d\\qquad(d\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, solving \\(\\ell_d\\) together with the inequalities defining \\(S_n\\)\ngives  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell_d\\cap S_n|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nand the maximum of (6) over all admissible integers \\(d\\) equals the\nright‑hand side of (4).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{M:=\\max_{\\text{sunny }L}|L\\cap S_n|\n      =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound for a non‑sunny line  \n\nA horizontal line (\\(b=c\\)), a vertical line (\\(a=c\\)) or a line parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) (\\(a+b=c\\)) meets the triangle \\(S_n\\) in a segment whose length\ndoes not exceed the side length \\(n\\).  Consequently each such line contains\nat most \\(n\\) lattice points of \\(S_n\\).  Thus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{every non‑sunny line contains }\\le n\\text{ points of }S_n } .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessary condition for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Using the bounds (7) and (8),\n\n\\[\nkM+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;N .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(N=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) and the value of \\(M\\) from (7).  A short\ncalculation yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible configuration must satisfy (10).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (10) there exists a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Notation for diagonal (slope‑1) lines  \n\nFor an integer \\(d\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\;a-b=d .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFormula (6) gives the number of points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(\\ell_d\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;a+b=c,\\qquad c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non‑sunny) and contains exactly\n\\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\); their union is precisely \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Maximal number of sunny lines  \n\n*Even \\(n=2t\\).*  \nUse the diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d\\qquad d=-t+1,-t+2,\\dots ,t-1 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nFor such a \\(d\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\ell_d\\cap S_n|=t-|d|+1 .\n\\]\n\nSumming over all \\(d\\) in (13) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=-(t-1)}^{t-1}(t-|d|+1)=2t^{2}+t=N .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(2t-1=n-1\\) diagonal lines already cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\nAdding any non‑sunny line (e.g. the vertical line \\(a=1\\)) yields a family\nof exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny ones.\n\n*Odd \\(n=2t+1\\).*  \nTake the diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d\\qquad d=-t,-t+1,\\dots ,t .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell_d\\cap S_n|=t+1-|d| ,\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=-t}^{t}(t+1-|d|)=(2t+1)(t+1)=N .\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(n\\) diagonal lines themselves form a required family with\n\\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.4  Intermediate values \\(0<k<k_{\\max}\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (10).  Define  \n\n\\[\nD_k:=\\Bigl\\{-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n            \\dots ,\n            \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n\\qquad |D_k|=k .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_d\\) with \\(d\\in D_k\\).\n\n*Points already covered.*  \nFrom (6),\n\n\\[\nC_k:=\\sum_{d\\in D_k}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n*Uncovered points.*  \nThe set \\(U:=S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{d\\in D_k}\\ell_d\\) consists of the points\nwith \\(a-b\\notin D_k\\).  For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the\nline \\(a+b=c\\) meets any diagonal line \\(\\ell_d\\) in at most one lattice\npoint; consequently it meets \\(U\\) in at most one point for each value of\n\\(d\\notin D_k\\).  Hence a line \\(a+b=c\\) can cover at most \\(|\\{d\\notin\nD_k\\}|\\) points of \\(U\\), but we do not need that many because the total\nsize of \\(U\\) is\n\n\\[\n|U|=N-C_k .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nUsing (6) one checks that for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n\\[\nN-C_k\\le (n-k)n .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, (18) is exactly inequality (9) with the maximal possible\ncontribution \\(M\\) of a sunny line; the left‑hand side of (18) is the\ndeficit after using the optimal \\(k\\) sunny lines.)\n\n*Selection of the non‑sunny lines.*  \nBecause each non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (see\n(8)), the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can together cover at most\n\\((n-k)n\\) points.  By (18) this capacity is at least \\(|U|\\), so we can\nchoose \\(n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines that together cover all points of\n\\(U\\).  An explicit convenient choice is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{for }c=2,3,\\dots ,n-k+1\\text{ take }a+b=c,\\\\\n&\\text{if after those }n-k\\text{ lines some points of }U\\text{ remain,\n  they all lie on }a+b=n+1,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nand we replace the last line \\(a+b=n-k+1\\) by the line \\(a+b=n+1\\) when\nnecessary.  Thus we never use more than \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nAll chosen lines are distinct because the sunny lines have slope \\(1\\) while\nthe non‑sunny lines have slopes \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\nConsequently we have produced, for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (10), a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) with exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nInequality (10) is necessary, and the constructions of Sections 4.2–4.4\nshow that it is also sufficient.  Therefore the set of admissible numbers\nof sunny lines is exactly the one stated in the verdict.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\n\\qquad \n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\ \\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}\\; } .\n\\]"
