d Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN:=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for a sunny line  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\nWrite a non‑vertical line in the form  \n\n\\[\np\\,a-q\\,b=r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;(p,q)=1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope is \\(p/q\\); the three forbidden slopes correspond to\n\\((p,q)=(0,1),(1,0),(-1,1)\\).  Hence for a sunny line we have\n\\(|p+q|\\ge2\\).\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct lattice points on the line,\nsubtracting the two equations in (2) gives  \n\n\\[\np\\,(a-a')=q\\,(b-b') .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((p,q)=1\\), the smallest non‑zero integer solution is\n\\((a-a',b-b')=(q,p)\\); therefore moving from one lattice point of the line\nto the next changes the value of \\(a+b\\) by \\(|p+q|\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently the number\nof points of \\(S_n\\) that can lie on the line is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) for a sunny line, the right‑hand side of (3) is bounded\nby  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe bound is attained by the lines of slope \\(1\\), i.e. by the equations  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\;a-b=d\\qquad(d\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, solving \\(\\ell_d\\) together with the inequalities defining \\(S_n\\)\ngives  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell_d\\cap S_n|\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nand the maximum of (6) over all admissible integers \\(d\\) equals the\nright‑hand side of (4).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{M:=\\max_{\\text{sunny }L}|L\\cap S_n|\n      =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound for a non‑sunny line  \n\nA horizontal line (\\(b=c\\)), a vertical line (\\(a=c\\)) or a line parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) (\\(a+b=c\\)) meets the triangle \\(S_n\\) in a segment whose length\ndoes not exceed the side length \\(n\\).  Consequently each such line contains\nat most \\(n\\) lattice points of \\(S_n\\).  Thus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{every non‑sunny line contains }\\le n\\text{ points of }S_n } .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessary condition for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Using the bounds (7) and (8),\n\n\\[\nkM+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;N .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(N=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) and the value of \\(M\\) from (7).  A short\ncalculation yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1,& n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,  & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible configuration must satisfy (10).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (10) there exists a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Notation for diagonal (slope‑1) lines  \n\nFor an integer \\(d\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\;a-b=d .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFormula (6) gives the number of points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(\\ell_d\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;a+b=c,\\qquad c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non‑sunny) and contains exactly\n\\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\); their union is precisely \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Maximal number of sunny lines  \n\n*Even \\(n=2t\\).*  \nUse the diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d\\qquad d=-t+1,-t+2,\\dots ,t-1 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nFor such a \\(d\\),\n\n\\[\n|\\ell_d\\cap S_n|=t-|d|+1 .\n\\]\n\nSumming over all \\(d\\) in (13) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=-(t-1)}^{t-1}(t-|d|+1)=2t^{2}+t=N .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(2t-1=n-1\\) diagonal lines already cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\nAdding any non‑sunny line (e.g. the vertical line \\(a=1\\)) yields a family\nof exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny ones.\n\n*Odd \\(n=2t+1\\).*  \nTake the diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d\\qquad d=-t,-t+1,\\dots ,t .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell_d\\cap S_n|=t+1-|d| ,\n\\]\n\nand  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d=-t}^{t}(t+1-|d|)=(2t+1)(t+1)=N .\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(n\\) diagonal lines themselves form a required family with\n\\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.4  Intermediate values \\(0<k<k_{\\max}\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy (10).  Define  \n\n\\[\nD_k:=\\Bigl\\{-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n            \\dots ,\n            \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n\\qquad |D_k|=k .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_d\\) with \\(d\\in D_k\\).\n\n*Points already covered.*  \nFrom (6),\n\n\\[\nC_k:=\\sum_{d\\in D_k}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n*Uncovered points.*  \nThe set \\(U:=S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{d\\in D_k}\\ell_d\\) consists of the points\nwith \\(a-b\\notin D_k\\).  For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the\nline \\(a+b=c\\) meets any diagonal line \\(\\ell_d\\) in at most one lattice\npoint; consequently it meets \\(U\\) in at most one point for each value of\n\\(d\\notin D_k\\).  Hence a line \\(a+b=c\\) can cover at most \\(|\\{d\\notin\nD_k\\}|\\) points of \\(U\\), but we do not need that many