r each \\(k\\) (claimed to work for all \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\n#### 3.1 Choice of a fixed non‑sunny line\n\n> “*Use the vertical line \\(\\ell_V:\\;a=1\\), which is non‑sunny and covers all points of the column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).*”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe line \\(a=1\\) is indeed vertical, hence non‑sunny, and it contains exactly the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le n\\). This part is correct.  \n**Conclusion:** *Correct.*\n\n#### 3.2 Reduction to \\(S_{n-1}\\)\n\n> “*Removing the column \\(a=1\\) from \\(S_n\\) and translating \\(a\\mapsto a-1\\) yields the set \\(S_{n-1}\\).*”\n\n**Verification:**  \nSubtracting 1 from the \\(a\\)-coordinate of every remaining point indeed maps the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) bijectively onto \\(\\{(a',b)\\mid a'\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a'+b\\le n\\}=S_{n-1}\\). This reduction is valid.  \n**Conclusion:** *Correct.*\n\n#### 3.3 Sunny diagonal lines \\(\\delta_d\\)\n\n> “*For an integer \\(d\\) define the diagonal (slope 1) line \\(\\delta_d:\\;a-b=d\\). Such a line is sunny because its direction vector \\((1,1)\\) is neither horizontal, vertical nor parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).*”\n\n**Verification:**  \nA line of slope 1 is indeed not parallel to the forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)). Hence each \\(\\delta_d\\) is sunny. The description of its intersection with the column \\(a=1\\) and the count \\(|\\delta_d\\cap S_{n-1}|=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-d}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) is correct (the points satisfy \\(b=a-d\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-d\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\)).  \n**Conclusion:** *Correct.*\n\n#### 3.4 Non‑sunny anti‑diagonal lines \\(\\alpha_c\\)\n\n> “*For an integer \\(c\\) define the anti‑diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)) line \\(\\alpha_c:\\;a+b=c\\). Every \\(\\alpha_c\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.*”\n\n**Verification:**  \nIndeed, lines of the form \\(a+b=c\\) have slope \\(-1\\) and are exactly the forbidden direction. Thus each \\(\\alp