 \\(x+y\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\ny_i\\le n+1-x_i\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n… Substituting the upper bounds (2) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-x_3)-(n+1-x_1)}{x_3-x_1}= \\frac{x_1-x_3}{x_3-x_1}= -1 .\n\\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe inequality \\(y_i\\le n+1-x_i\\) is correct, but the proof **treats it as an equality** for the points that lie on the line. In general the points on a line need not lie on the boundary \\(y=n+1-x\\); they may be strictly below it. Replacing each \\(y_i\\) by the upper bound \\(n+1-x_i\\) therefore changes the slope computation. The derived slope \\(-1\\) is not forced; the line could have any slope consistent with the actual coordinates. Hence the substitution is invalid.  \n\n**Status:** **Critical Error** – the step is mathematically unsound.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Consequence: “a sunny line can contain at most two points”\n\n**Quote:**  \n“Consequently a **sunny** line (i.e. a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThis conclusion follows directly from the false Lemma 1. As shown above, the sunny line \\(y=x\\) can contain many points of \\(T_n\\) (up to \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\)). Therefore the claim that a sunny line can contain at most two points is **incorrect**.  \n\n**Status:** **Critical Error** – the bound on the number of points per sunny line is false.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Upper bound on the total number of points covered\n\n**Quote:**  \n“Suppose we have \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2k + n\\,(n-k).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe term \\(2k\\) assumes each sunny line contributes at most two distinct points, which we have just shown to be false. Consequently the estimate (5) is **unreliable**; a sunny line may contribute many more points, so the “at most” statement is not justified.  \n\n**Status:** **Critical Error** – the capacity estimate is based on a false premise.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Derivation of inequality (6) and bound (8)\n\n**Quote:**  \n“Since \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\), we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le 2k+n(n-k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (6) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n… Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 3 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nBecause inequality (5) is invalid, the derived inequality (6) does not hold in general. The subsequent algebraic manipulation is correct **if** (6) were true, but since its premise is false, the conclusion \\(k\\le3\\) is **unsubstantiated**. In fact, for many values of \\(n\\) one can construct configurations with more than three sunny lines (e.g., using many lines of slope \\(1\\) that each pass through several points).  \n\n**Status:** **Critical Error** – the bound on \\(k\\) is derived from an incorrect inequality.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Construction of configurations – definition of the families\n\n**Quote:**  \n“*Common non‑sunny line.*  \nThe vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_V:\\;x=1\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nis non‑sunny and contains the whole first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).\n\nAfter removing this column and translating \\(x\\mapsto x-1\\) the remaining points form the set \\(T_{n-1}\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor an integer \\(d\\) define  \n\n\\[\ns_d:\\;y=x+d .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector \\((1,1)\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore each \\(s_d\\) is sunny.  \nThe line \\(s_d\\) meets \\(T_{n-1}\\) exactly in the two points  \n\n\\[\n(1-d,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (n-d-1,n-1),\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nprovided \\(0\\le d\\le n-2\\); in particular it always contains **exactly two** points of \\(T_{n-1}\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nThe translation argument that the remaining points after removing the first column form \\(T_{n-1}\\) is correct. However, the claim about the intersection of \\(s_d\\) with \\(T_{n-1}\\) is **false**. In the coordinates of \\(T_{n-1}\\) (where the new \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(x' = a-1\\)), the line \\(y = x + d\\) becomes \\(y = x' + (d+1)\\). Solving \\(y = x' + (d+1)\\) together with the constraints \\(x'\\ge1\\), \\(y\\ge1\\), \\(x'+y\\le n\\) yields many integer solutions: for each integer \\(x'\\) satisfying \\(1\\le x'\\le \\lfloor (n-d-1)/2\\rfloor\\) we obtain a point \\((x',x'+d+1)\\). Hence the line typically contains **more than two** points (e.g., for \\(d=0\\) it contains \\((1,1), (2,2),\\dots\\)). The specific points \\((1-d,1)\\) and \\((n-d-1,n-1)\\) are not even guaranteed to belong to the set (the first has \\(x\\)-coordinate possibly non‑positive). Therefore the statement “exactly two points” is incorrect.  \n\n**Status:** **Critical Error** – the description of the sunny lines’ intersection with \\(T_{n-1}\\) is wrong.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Definition of the full family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)\n\n**Quote:**  \n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\ell_V\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{s_d\\mid d=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{a_c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n-k+1\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n**Analysis:**  \nEven if the families were correctly described, the previous error about the number of points covered by each \\(s_d\\) invalidates the claim that the total number of points covered by the sunny lines is exactly \\(2k\\). Consequently the counting argument that follows cannot be trusted.  \n\n**Status:** Dependent on previous errors – **Critical Error** propagates.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Counting of lines and claim of coverage\n\n**Quote:**  \n“The set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n1\\;+\\;k\\;+\\;(n-k)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(s_d\\) are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nThe line \\(\\ell_V\\) covers the whole first column of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(s_d\\) each cover two points of the remaining triangle \\(T_{n-1}\\); altogether they cover \\(2k\\) distinct points.  \nThe \\(n-k\\) anti‑diagonal lines \\(a_c\\) together cover all points of \\(T_{n-1}\\) that are not already covered by the sunny lines (each such line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points, and a straightforward counting argument shows that the total capacity \\((n-k)(n-1)\\) is at least the number of uncovered points).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The count of lines (1 + k + (n‑k) = n) is correct **provided** the families are indeed disjoint, which they are (vertical line, lines of slope 1, and lines of slope –1).  \n- The claim that each sunny line covers exactly two points is false (see point 7). Hence the statement “altogether they cover \\(2k\\) distinct points” is incorrect.  \n- The “straightforward counting argument” for the anti‑diagonal lines is not presented; moreover, because the sunny lines may already cover many points, the remaining uncovered set is not simply counted by subtracting \\(2k\\). Overlaps between anti‑diagonal lines are also ignored, so the capacity estimate \\((n-k)(n-1)\\) does not guarantee coverage. This is a **justification gap** at minimum, but combined with the false premise about the sunny lines it becomes a **critical error**.  \n\n**Status:** **Critical Error** (due to false premise) and **Justification Gap** (lack of rigorous covering argument).\n\n---\n\n### 10. Conclusion\n\n**Quote:**  \n“Lemma 1 together with the counting inequality (6) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines.  \nThe explicit construction (13) shows that each of the values \\(0,1,2,3\\) is attainable.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,3\\}} .\n\\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \nSince Lemma 1 is false and the counting inequality (6) is derived from an invalid capacity bound, the impossibility claim for \\(k\\ge4\\) is unsupported. Moreover, the construction fails to produce configurations with the claimed numbers of sunny lines because the sunny lines do not behave as asserted. Therefore the final answer is not justified.  \n\n**Status:** **Critical Error** – the conclusion rests on multiple earlier critical errors.\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution’s core argument hinges on Lemma 1, which is false. Consequently, the derived upper bound \\(k\\le3\\) and the subsequent construction are both invalid. The proof contains several **Critical Errors** that break the logical chain, and the few remaining steps suffer from **Justification Gaps** (e.g., the unproved covering argument for anti‑diagonal lines). Because the fundamental claims are incorrect, the solution does **not** correctly solve the problem."
