            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;}\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad \n\\boxed{\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n+1}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n-1\\;}\n\\]\n\n(the value \\(k=n\\) occurs only for the exceptional case \\(n=3\\)).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound for sunny lines.**  \n   A sunny line meets each anti‑diagonal  \n   \\(\\Delta_c:=\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=c\\}\\) in at most one point.  \n   The anti‑diagonal \\(\\Delta_{\\,n+1}\\) contains \\(n\\) points; therefore at least one\n   anti‑diagonal line (which is not sunny) is needed when \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n   Hence for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Necessary lower bound.**  \n   Let \\(t=n-k\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines.  \n   Choose the \\(t\\) anti‑diagonal lines\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x+y=i+1\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,t) .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   They cover all points whose sum is \\(\\le t+1\\).  \n   The still uncovered points lie on the remaining anti‑diagonals\n   \\(\\Delta_{t+2},\\dots ,\\Delta_{n+1}\\); there are exactly \\(k\\) of them.\n   Their total number is\n   \\[\n   U=\\sum_{j=t+1}^{n}j\n     =\\frac{k\\bigl(2n-k+1\\bigr)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   Each sunny line can meet each of the \\(k\\) uncovered anti‑diagonals in at most\n   one point, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k^{2}\\) of those points.\n   Consequently\n   \\[\n   U\\le k^{2}\\Longleftrightarrow\n   \\frac{k\\bigl(2n-k+1\\bigr)}2\\le k^{2}\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   3k\\ge 2n+1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Hence, for \\(k>0\\),\n   \\[\n   k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n+1}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Existence for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (6) and, when \\(n\\ge4\\), also (2).  \n   Put \\(t=n-k\\) and keep the \\(t\\) anti‑diagonal lines (3).  \n   It remains to exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of the\n   uncovered anti‑diagonals \\(\\Delta_{t+2},\\dots ,\\Delta_{n+1}\\).\n\n   *Construction of the sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) consider the set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_r=\\Bigl\\{\\,P_{r,c}\\mid c=t+r+1,\\;c\\in\\{t+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\Bigr\\},\n   \\qquad\n   P_{r,c}:=\\bigl(r,\\;c-r\\bigr).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The point \\(P_{r,c}\\) lies on the anti‑diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\) because\n   \\(r+(c-r)=c\\), and it belongs to \\(T_n\\) because\n   \\(1\\le r\\le k\\le n-1\\) and \\(1\\le c-r\\le n\\).  \n   For a fixed \\(r\\) the points \\(P_{r,c}\\) (as \\(c\\) runs through the\n   uncovered anti‑diagonals) are **collinear**: indeed,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{c_2-r-(c_1-r)}{c_2-c_1}=1,\n   \\]\n   so the line through any two of them has slope \\(-1\\).  \n   To avoid the forbidden slope \\(-1\\) we *perturb* each set by adding a\n   different integer offset to the second coordinate:\n\n   \\[\n   Q_{r,c}:=\\bigl(r,\\;c-r+r\\cdot (r-1)\\bigr)\n          =\\bigl(r,\\;c+r(r-2)\\bigr).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The points \\(Q_{r,c}\\) still lie on \\(\\Delta_c\\) because\n   \\(r+(c+r(r-2))=c+r(r-1