or every admissible \\(k\\) (construction of sunny lines)\n\n##### 4.1 Definition of the sets \\(S_r\\) and points \\(P_{r,c}\\)\n> \u201cFor each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) consider the set  \n> \\[\n> S_r=\\Bigl\\{\\,P_{r,c}\\mid c=t+r+1,\\;c\\in\\{t+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\Bigr\\},\n> \\qquad\n> P_{r,c}:=\\bigl(r,\\;c-r\\bigr).\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Analysis:*  \n- Each \\(P_{r,c}\\) indeed lies on the anti\u2011diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\) because \\(r+(c-r)=c\\).  \n- For a fixed \\(r\\) the points \\(P_{r,c}\\) all have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(r\\); they are collinear on the **vertical** line \\(x=r\\).  \n- A vertical line is **not sunny** (it is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  The construction therefore does not yet satisfy the requirement that the final lines be sunny.\n\n*Result:* **Justification Gap** \u2013 the author recognises the problem (\u201cTo avoid the forbidden slope \\(-1\\) we *perturb* \u2026\u201d) but does not provide a valid way to turn a vertical line into a sunny one while preserving the covering property.\n\n##### 4.2 Perturbation to obtain \\(Q_{r,c}\\)\n> \u201cWe *perturb* each set by adding a different integer offset to the second coordinate:  \n> \\[\n> Q_{r,c}:=\\bigl(r,\\;c-r+r\\cdot (r-1)\\bigr)\n>          =\\bigl(r,\\;c+r(r-2)\\bigr).\n> \\]  \n> The points \\(Q_{r,c}\\) still lie on \\(\\Delta_c\\) because  \n> \\[\n> r+(c+r(r-2))=c+r(r-1)\\equiv c\\pmod{1}.\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Analysis:*  \n- The algebraic identity is **incorrect**: \\(r+(c+r(r-2)) = c + r(r-1)\\), which equals \\(c\\) **only modulo 1**.  The condition for a point to belong to \\(\\Delta_c\\) is the exact equality \\(x+y=c\\), not equality modulo 1.  Hence \\(Q_{r,c}\\) lies on the anti\u2011diagonal \\(\\Delta_{c+r(r-1)}\\), not on \\(\\Delta_c\\).  \n- Consequently the perturbed points no longer belong to the required anti\u2011diagonals, and the whole covering argument collapses.\n\n*Result:* **Critical Error** \u2013 the fundamental claim that the perturbed points remain on the same anti\u2011diagonal is false.\n\n##### 4.3 Claim of collinearity and slope of \\(\\ell_r\\)\n> \u201cThe points \\(\\{Q_{r,c}\\mid c=t+r+1,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) are no longer collinear; \u2026 the unique line \\(\\ell_r\\) passing through the two extreme points \u2026 meets each intermediate anti\u2011diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\) in exactly one of the points \\(Q_{r,c}\\).  \n> By construction the slope of \\(\\ell_r\\) is  \n> \\[\n> m_r=\\frac{(n+1)+r(r-2)-\\bigl(t+r+1+r(r-2)\\bigr)}{(n+1)- (t+r+1)}\n>        =\\frac{n-t}{n-t}=1,\n> \\]  \n> i.e. \\(m_r=1\\) for every \\(r\\).\u201d\n\n*Analysis:*  \n- Because the points \\(Q_{r,c}\\) are **not** on the same anti\u2011diagonal, there is no reason for them to be collinear.  In fact, for generic \\(r\\) they are not collinear at all.  \n- The slope computation uses the coordinates of the *extreme* points \\(Q_{r,t+r+1}\\) and \\(Q_{r,n+1}\\).  Substituting the (incorrect) expressions for those points yields the algebraic simplification shown, but the simplification is based on the false premise that both points lie on the same line \\(\\ell_r\\) that also passes through the intermediate \\(Q_{r,c}\\).  Even if the algebra were correct, the resulting slope \\(1\\) corresponds to a line **parallel to the line \\(y=x\\)**, which is sunny, but the derivation is invalid because the points are not collinear.  \n\n*Result:* **Critical Error** \u2013 the claimed existence of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_r\\) is unsupported; the construction is mathematically incorrect.\n\n##### 4.4 Distinctness of the \\(\\ell_r\\)\n> \u201cThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct because they have different \\(x\\)-intercepts \u2026\u201d\n\n*Analysis:*  \n- This statement depends on the previous (flawed) construction.  Since the lines \\(\\ell_r\\) have not been correctly defined, the argument for their distinctness is meaningless.\n\n*Result:* **Critical Error** \u2013 follows from the earlier invalid construction.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. The case \\(k=0\\)\n> \u201cTaking the \\(n\\) anti\u2011diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) gives a configuration with no sunny line.\u201d\n\n*Analysis:*  \n- This is a correct observation: each of those lines is parallel to \\(\\Delta_c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), hence non\u2011sunny, and together they cover every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n*Result:* **Correct**.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n> \u201cThe three lines  \n> \\[\n> y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+ \\tfrac52\n> \\]  \n> are all sunny and together contain the six points; thus \\(k=3\\) is also admissible when \\(n=3\\).\u201d\n\n*Analysis:*  \n- The line \\(y=x\\) covers \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \n- The line \\(y=2x-1\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) again but **does not** pass through any of the remaining points \\((1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \n- The line \\(y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\) covers \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) but leaves \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) uncovered.  \n- Hence the three listed lines **do not** cover all six points.  A correct covering exists (e.g. \\(y=x,\\;y=-2x+5,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\)), but the solution\u2019s justification is wrong.\n\n*Result:* **Justification Gap** \u2013 the provided example fails to demonstrate the claimed covering.\n\n---\n\n#### 7. Overall Conclusion\nThe solution correctly identifies the necessary lower bound \\(k\\ge\\lceil(2n+1)/3\\rceil\\) and correctly handles the trivial case \\(k=0\\).  However, it **fails** to establish a valid upper bound for \\(k\\) (the argument for \\(k\\le n-1\\) is unsound) and the **construction** that is supposed to show that every \\(k\\) satisfying the lower bound can be realized is mathematically incorrect.  Moreover, the illustrative example for \\(n=3\\) is erroneous.  Because the core existence claim rests on a flawed construction, the answer does not solve the problem.\n\n**Therefore the solution is invalid.**"

