 \n> Without loss of generality we may replace the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines by the \\(t\\) anti‑diagonal lines (3); this can only increase the set of points they cover, so any covering with \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines can be transformed into a covering with the same number of anti‑diagonal lines.  \n> After removing those \\(t\\) anti‑diagonal lines, the uncovered points lie on the \\(k\\) anti‑diagonals \\(\\Delta_{t+2},\\dots ,\\Delta_{n+1}\\). Their total number is  \n> \\[\n> U=\\sum_{c=t+2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{k\\bigl(2n-k+1\\bigr)}2 .\n> \\tag{8}\n> \\]  \n> Each sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the \\(k\\) anti‑diagonals, therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k^{2}\\) points. Hence \\(U\\le k^{2}\\), i.e.  \n> \\[\n> \\frac{k\\bigl(2n-k+1\\bigr)}2\\le k^{2}\n> \\Longleftrightarrow 3k\\ge 2n+1 .\n> \\tag{9}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification of the claim “replace the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines by the \\(t\\) anti‑diagonal lines (3) … can only increase the set of points they cover”:**  \n\n- Non‑sunny lines are of three possible types: horizontal (\\(y=b\\)), vertical (\\(x=a\\)), or anti‑diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n- An anti‑diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) covers **all** points of the single anti‑diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\) (i.e. exactly \\(c-1\\) points).  \n- A horizontal line \\(y=b\\) covers the points \\((a,b)\\) for all admissible \\(a\\) (i.e. \\(a=1,\\dots,n+1-b\\)). These points lie on **different** anti‑diagonals: each point belongs to a distinct \\(\\Delta_{a+b}\\). Consequently a horizontal line can cover up to \\(n\\) points, spread over many anti‑diagonals.  \n- Replacing a horizontal (or vertical) line by an anti‑diagonal line generally **reduces** the number of covered points. Simple counterexample (with \\(n=3\\)): the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) covers the three points \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\). Replacing it by the anti‑diagonal line \\(x+y=4\\) covers only the three points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\). The set of covered points is not a superset; indeed \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) are lost.  \n\nThus the “without loss of generality” claim is **false**. The transformation can **decrease** coverage, so the subsequent counting of uncovered points (formula (8)) is not guaranteed to hold for an arbitrary optimal configuration of non‑sunny lines.\n\nBecause the entire derivation of inequality (9) rests on this faulty replacement, the inequality itself is **not rigorously established**. Consequently the claimed necessary condition \\(3k\\ge 2n+1\\) lacks a valid proof.\n\n**Conclusion:** This step contains a **Critical Error** that invalidates the lower‑bound argument.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4 – Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n> “The \\(n\\) anti‑diagonal lines (6) are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The anti‑diagonal lines are precisely the lines \\(x+y=c\\) for \\(c=2,3,\\dots,n+1\\).  \n- By definition each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for some \\(c\\) in that range, so it lies on the corresponding anti‑diagonal line.  \n- All these lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \n\n**Conclusion:** The statement is correct and fully justified.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 – Existence for the special case \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\)\n> “The three lines listed in (7) are sunny (their slopes are \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\;2\\)) and together contain the six points of \\(T_3\\); therefore \\(k=3\\) is attainable when \\(n=3\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The solution references “the three lines listed in (7)”, but the actual equations of those lines are **not provided** in the text.  \n- Without explicit equations, we cannot verify that (i) the lines are indeed sunny (i.e. not parallel to the forbidden directions), (ii) they are distinct, and (iii) their union covers all six points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le4\\).  \n\n**Conclusion:** This is a **Justification Gap**: the existence claim is plausible but